      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Принеси ему немного кофе.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'hello' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Доброе утро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good morning.' Notice how 'good' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'morning'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, вас ожидают.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, they are waiting for you.' The character links 'chef' directly with 'you' to express the thought clearly. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Кто меня ждет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Who is waiting for me?' Chef Viktor combines 'who' with 'wait for' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Дима тебя ждет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Dima is waiting for you.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'Dima' with 'wait for'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Нагиев здесь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nagiyev is here?' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'Nagiyev' straight to 'here'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, он за своим столом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, he is at his table.' Notice how 'yes' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'the table'. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Что он хочет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What does he want?' Chef Viktor links 'what' directly with 'he' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I do not know.' The character combines 'I' with 'know' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Обычно он сначала звонит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He usually calls first.' Notice how 'usually' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'is calling'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он кажется очень взволнованным.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He seems very excited.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'it seems', which directs the action back onto the subject. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Хорошо, я иду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Okay, I am coming.' Chef Viktor links 'good' directly with 'I' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "viktor",
      "t": "Принеси ему немного кофе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Bring him some coffee.' Chef Viktor links 'bring' directly with 'him' to express the thought clearly. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
